-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ACL-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ACLs work for both routers and switches

ACL (Access Control List)
	> Used to filter network traffic, based on layer 3 and layer 4 headers of packets, to enforce security boundaries and access policies
	> ACLs are created globally, then applied to interfaces
	> When a packet recieved at interface, if ACL applied on interface, based on the result of ACL, router/switch will permit or deny packet forward
	> ACL = Series of ACEs
	> ACE (Access Control Entry)
		> Single permit/deny rule
		> Each ACE written in separate line/command
	> ACL types
		1. Standard ACL
			> Constraints within ACEs
				source ip address only
			> ACE format: <action> <srcIP> <srcWildcard>
			> Default wildcard mask = 0.0.0.0, if no srcWildcard provided
		2. Extended ACL
			> Constraints within ACEs
				> layer 4 protocol
				> source ip address
				> source port number
				> destination ip address
				> destination port number
			> ACE format: <action> <protocol> <srcIP> <srcWildcard> <srcPort> <dstIP> <dstWildcard> <dstPort> <finalOptions>
			> No default wildcard masks, srcWildcard and dstWildcard mandatory
	> ACL syntax types
		1. Numbered ACL
			> Older syntax
			> 'access-list' command
			> Numbered Standard ACL syntax
				#access-list <ACLno> <action> <srcIP> <srcWildcard>
			> Numbered Extended ACL syntax
				#access-list <ACLno> <action> <layer4Protocol> <srcIP> <srcWildcard> <srcPort> <dstIP> <dstWildcard> <dstPort> <finalOptions>
		2. Named ACL
			> Newer syntax
			> 'ip access-list' command
			> Named Standard ACL syntax
				R1(config)#ip access-list standard <ACLname>
				R1(config-std-nacl)#<action> <srcIP> <srcWildcard>
			> Named Extended ACL syntax
				R1(config)#ip access-list extended <ACLname>
				R1(config-ext-nacl)#<action> <protocol> <srcIP> <srcWildcard> <srcPort> <dstIP> <dstWildcard> <destPort> <finalOptions>
		> An ACL can be referenced using its number or name, for ACL modification or creating access-groups
	> ACE Parameters
		> ACL NUMBER: 1-99 (std ACL) | 100-199 (extd ACL) | 1300-1999 (std ACL) | 2000-2699 (extd ACL)
		> SEQUENCENUMBER: set sequence number for that ACE, manipulating the position of ACE in ACL
		> ACTION: "permit" | "deny" | "remark"		// "remark" to add description to the ace
		> LAYER 4 PROTOCOL: "tcp" | "udp" | "ip" | "icmp" | "ospf" | ... (tcp/udp -> application traffic; ospf -> routing traffic; ip -> all traffic)
		> SOURCE ADDRESS: <srcIP> <wildcard> | any | host <srcIP>
		> SOURCE PORT *optional (defaults to any)*: eq | gt | neq | lt <portNumber>
		> DESTINATION ADDRESS: <dstIP> <wildcard> | any | host <dstIP>
		> DESTINATION PORT *optional (defaults to any)*: <"eq" | "gt" | "neq" | "lt"> <portNumber | portName>	// std ports:80<->http 443<->https 23<->telnet
		> FINAL OPTIONS: "log" (for logging to console), other options for tcp flags, etc
	> ACE Order within ACL
		> ACEs have sequence numbers
		> Inside an ACL, ACEs are organised in ascending order of their sequence numbers
		> Hence, ACE higher in ACL is executed over ACE lower in ACL
		> When a packet is to be checked against an ACL, the ACL is read from top to bottom, and only the first matched ACE will be executed
		> Lower sequence number => Higher execution preference
		> Sequence number on an ACE
			> Implicitly set in increments of 10, when explicitly not set
			> Explicitly set (for sequence manipulation in ACL)
				R1(config)#ip access-list <"standard" | "extended"> <ACLname or number>
				R1(config-ext-nacl)#<sequenceNumber> <action> <protocol> <srcIP> <srcWc> <srcPort> <dstIP> <dstWc> <dstPort> <finalOptions>
		> Specificity Order: specific ACEs to be placed above broader ACEs (else broader ACEs will always get matched and executed => specific ACEs never get matched)
		> To input ACEs with explicitly set sequential numbers in a Numbered ACL, use Named ACL syntax with the ACL_number as the ACL_name
	> Access-Groups
		> ACLs are applied to interfaces with the "access-group" command at the interface level
		> At an interface, ACLs are applied in inbound and outbound directions
		> Maximum of 1 ACL per direction per interface => Maximum of 2 ACLs at an interface
		> Syntax: R1(config-if)#ip access-group <ACLname or number> <"in" | "out">		// set inbound or outbound ACL on interface
	> Implicit Deny All Rule
		> There is an implicit 'deny any any' rule at the bottom of all ACLs
		> If an ACL is not applied to an interface, all traffic is allowed
		> If an ACL is applied to an interface, all traffic is denied except what is explicitly allowed
		> An explicit deny all rule can be included at the end of ACL, to log illegal traffic
		> If reverse behaviour is required, a 'permit any any' rule can be included at the end of ACL
			=> It will always still be above the implicit deny all rule, hence always superceding it


---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ACL_LAB---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Config ACL (Creating ACL)
	1. Numbered ACL syntax
		1. Standard ACL
			R1(config)#access-list <ACLno.> <action> <srcIP> <srcWildcard>								// Standard ACL: 1-99 or 1300-1999
		2. Extended ACL
			R1(config)#access-list <ACLno.> <action> <protocol> <srcIP> <srcWc> <srcPort> <dstIP> <dstWc> <dstPort> <finalOp>	// Extended ACL: 100-199 or 2000-2699
	2. Named ACL syntax
		1. Standard ACL
			R1(config)#ip access-list standard <ACLname>
			R1(config-std-nacl)#<sequenceNumber> <action> <srcIP> <srcWc>
		2. Extended ACL
			R1(config)#ip access-list extended <ACLname>
			R1(config-ext-nacl)#<sequenceNumber> <action> <protocol> <srcIP> <srcWc> <srcPort> <dstIP> <dstWc> <dstPort> <finalOp>
	// Optional parameters - sequenceNumber, srcPort, dstPort

Verification ACL
	1. R1#show access-lists						// for all ACLs, shows ACL type, ACEs within ACL, no of packets matched to each ACE
	2. R1#show access-lists <ACLno>					// for the specified ACL, shows ACL type, ACEs within the ACL, no. of packets matched to each ACE

Config Access-Group (Applying ACL to interface)
	R1(config)#interface f0/0
	R1(config-if)#ip access-group <ACLno/name> <"in" | "out">	// set inbound or outbound ACL on interface

Verification Access-Group
	1. R1#show interface f0/0 | include access list			// specifies the ACL numbers (or "not set") for both inbound and outbound on interface